  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 225 PM MDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to be in support of ridging at \n500-mb overhead through the duration of the long term period. At\nthe same time, a 500-mb shortwave trough looks to be traveling \nanticyclonically around the ridge, with a high amplitude 850-mb \ntrough at its leading edge. There are indications that a surface\nlow will be able to form across the High Plains in association \nwith this 850-mb feature, and move over or just south of the \nforecast region Saturday and Sunday.\n\n***Saturday/Sunday***\n\nSoutherly flow underneath the ridge alongside solar heating \nlooks to favor hot conditions Saturday and Sunday, with highs \nforecast in the mid to upper 90s, and possibly low 100s in some \nspots. In addition, GEFS 850-mb height guidance is in support of\na high across portions of Eastern Texas during this period, \nwhich would prevent moisture from entering the southerly return \nflow. This is expected to promote dry conditions across portions\nof East-Central Colorado and West- Central Kansas, where \nrelative humidities (RH) may drop into the lower 20s to upper \nteens. While RH values are forecast to be a bit low, fire \nweather conditions are not yet expected either Saturday or \nSunday, as wind gusts are not favored to exceed 25 mph except in\nKit Carson and Yuma Counties in Colorado, where wind gusts may \nreach the 25-30 mph range. Still, LREF guidance shows that RH \nvalues have less than a 20% chance of meeting criteria for \ncritical fire weather conditions (less than 15% RH) across \nnearly the entire forecast area. Confidence in a Red Flag \nWarning being needed is less than 5% both Saturday and Sunday. \n\nForecast guidance continues to suggest that showers and \nthunderstorms are also possible during this period in \nassociation with convergence from the surface low pressure. Even\nso, NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance shows a 1 in 3 chance or \nless across the forecast area both days for greater than 0.1 \ninches of precipitation to be experienced. Any storms that \ndevelop are not expected to be severe, as LREF guidance shows a \n2 in 3 chance or better that sfc- 500mb wind shear will be below\n30 kts. Currently, the greatest concern with these showers is \nthat they may be able to produce dry lightning, particularly \nacross East-Central Colorado where RH values are lowest, and NBM\nguidance shows the highest chances of showers occurring.\n\n***Monday/Tuesday***\n\nAs the surface low moves well south of the forecast area, a \nweak cold front may lower temperatures a couple of degrees into \nthe low to mid 90s. Chances for precipitation increase a little \nbit during this period, especially on Monday when NBM 24 hr \nprecipitation guidance shows a 40-60% chance of receiving \ngreater than 0.1 inches of rain across portions of Eastern \nColorado. This would be in association with a cold front. The \nrisk for severe storms continues to be low, as LREF guidance \nshows similar probabilities for wind shear as Saturday and \nSunday. RH values look to improve as well, as temperatures drop \na little, and easterly winds across portions of Central and East\nKansas feed slightly higher dew points into the forecast area. \nThis would reduce risks for critical fire weather.",
